,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],"limit":3},"output":5000}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def numRescueBoats(self, people: List[int], limit: int) -> int:\n        people.sort()\n        light, heavy = 0, len(people) - 1\n        boats = 0\n        while light <= heavy:\n            if people[light] + people[heavy] <= limit:\n                light += 1  # the lightest rides with the heaviest\n            heavy -= 1      # the heaviest always leaves on this boat\n            boats += 1\n        return boats\n","complexity":{"time":"O(n log n)","space":"O(1) beyond the sort"},"approach":"**The heaviest remaining person must board some boat now, and the best possible companion is the lightest remaining person.** Sort, then use two pointers: if lightest + heaviest fits, send both; otherwise the heaviest goes alone (nobody lighter than the lightest exists to pair with). Each iteration launches one boat and removes the heaviest, so the loop runs at most n times.\n\n**Exchange argument:** let `H` be the heaviest and `l` the lightest remaining, with `H + l <= limit`. Take any optimal solution in which `H` rides with some `x != l` and `l` rides with `y` (or alone). Swap partners to form `(H, l)` and `(x, y)`. The first boat fits by assumption. For the second, `y <= H` because `H` is the heaviest, so `x + y <= x + H <= limit`. The boat count is unchanged, so some optimal solution contains the greedy pair. If instead `H + l > limit`, nobody can share with `H`, so every solution sends `H` alone. Either way the greedy step is safe, and induction finishes the proof.\n\n**Common mistakes:** trying to fill boats greedily from the light end (leaves heavy people stranded one per boat when they could have shared), allowing more than two per boat, and forgetting that `light == heavy` still needs a boat.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Boats that carry up to k people? (Greedy no longer works in general; it becomes bin packing, NP-hard, so discuss heuristics.) Counting sort when weights are bounded? (O(n + limit), avoids the log factor.) Minimise the number of boats and then the total weight imbalance? (Secondary objective; the greedy pairs already balance well but you would need to prove or adjust.)","similar":["two-sum-ii-sorted","assign-cookies","two-city-scheduling"],"number":145,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def numRescueBoats(self, people: List[int], limit: int) -> int:\n        pass\n"}